====================================================================================================
  THE GLUED `Argument:` LABEL, AND WHETHER THE ROUND RAISED IT  [FORMAT INSTRUMENT]
====================================================================================================
A turn whose PUBLISHED argument still contains an `Argument:` label of its own:
the model wrote the label mid-sentence after some planning text, and
`parse_debater_output` — which takes the last label at a LINE START — published
the planning text as part of the public argument. The parser cannot catch it and
a reader of the record can see it, so the question is not whether it happens but
whether THIS round does it more than the debate it continues. The same count is
therefore taken over the PARENT rounds 1-3 of the very same cells, out of each
arm's own copy of them — nothing here opens `jd3-main`.

arm                           round-4 turns   glued           rate   parent turns   glued           rate
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
jd6 R — the contest round              1752     187 187/1752 10.7%           5376     571 571/5376 10.6%
jd6 B — the plain round                1772     134  134/1772 7.6%           5316     568 568/5316 10.7%
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A round-4 rate at or below its own parent rate is the habit INHERITED; a higher
one is the round RAISING it, and that would be a fact about the new prompts.

====================================================================================================
  THE SAME FOUR INSTRUMENTS, SPLIT BY STANCE  [arm R only]
====================================================================================================
PRO argues the alleged defects are real and material; ANTI argues they are not.
They are different tasks with different amounts to say, and a systematic
difference between them is a difference in what the judge reads on each side of
the objection — which is exactly the asymmetry the exchange block's symmetric
discount was rewritten to avoid leaning on. Smoke 1's two heavy word overruns
(441 and 687 against a 400-word limit) were BOTH PRO turns, which is why this
split is pre-registered rather than looked at afterwards.

