====================================================================================================
  TRUNCATED ROUND-4 TURNS  [the cell-loss mechanism]
====================================================================================================
`finish_reason == "length"` at `generation_max_tokens`: the turn ran out of
budget mid-argument, which is FATAL and unretryable — a truncated argument would
enter the public transcript as if authored — so the cell fails and is counted.
It is the sweep's own failure mode (a restart loop in the private Thinking
block), not a new one, and the arms report the loss rather than hiding it.

  jd6 R — the contest round   0 of 1752 completed round-4 turns (0/1752 0.0%)
  jd6 B — the plain round     0 of 1772 completed round-4 turns (0/1772 0.0%)

READ THIS COUNT WITH THE ATTEMPTS TABLE OF SECTION (0), NOT INSTEAD OF IT.
A TRUNCATED TURN IS NEVER COMMITTED — `_complete_with_repair` raises rather than
letting a cut-off argument enter the public transcript as if authored — so it
leaves NO row on disk to carry `finish_reason = "length"`. What it leaves is a
HALF-ROUND: the other debater's turn, committed before the raise, beside a run
whose manifest says failed. So this count reads 0 on the very cells it is about,
and the truncations are visible in three other places instead: the failed-cell
list in section (0) with the error verbatim, the `turns_n != 2` line above, and
the difference between 896 and each arm's `decided` column. The count below
catches the OTHER shape — a turn the provider marked `length` and still
returned parseably — which has not been seen in either smoke.

====================================================================================================
THE PRE-REGISTERED READING
====================================================================================================
P1  on the 583 initially-CORRECT cells decided by both arms: R broke 176 that B did not, B broke 62 that R did not, p = 7.888e-14.
    R BREAKS MORE — SIGNIFICANT at alpha=0.05
P2  on the 263 initially-WRONG cells decided by both arms: R fixed 98 that B did not, B fixed 35 that R did not, p = 4.292e-08.
    R FIXES AT LEAST AS MANY

THE RESULT IS A SPLIT AND IS REPORTED AS ONE. P1 FAILS and P2 HOLDS: the
contest round is MORE INTERVENTIONIST IN BOTH DIRECTIONS — it breaks more of
the decisions M0 got right AND fixes more of the ones it got wrong. That is
none of the four named outcomes: (A) needs P1, (B) needs R to break fewer,
(C) needs B to beat R on both, (D) needs no separation. PREREG.md's rule is
that a split is reported as the split it is, with both tests' numbers, and
NOT rounded to whichever named outcome it is nearest. It is not rounded here.

THE FOUR NAMED OUTCOMES, written into PREREG.md before either arm ran, so that
no rule is invented after the table:
  (A) P1 and P2 hold          the objection makes the extra round more
                              discriminating than an un-steered one
  (B) R breaks fewer AND      the contest round is CONSERVATIVE, not
      fixes fewer             discriminating
  (C) B beats R on both       the objection is worse than no objection
  (D) no separation           the round's content does not matter, only that
                              there was one

WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CLAIM: nothing about jd3's P1, `single`/`self_critique`,
natural-error selection, `weak_alone`, or the same-model property (Maverick
judged these debates and rules on the appeals — the jd3 design, unrepaired here).
No number here is pooled with jd3-jd5's: the ruling prompt and the pin differ.
